          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Hawthorne's Use of Symbolism</strong><br>\nHawthorne extensively used symbolism in his works. \"The Scarlet Letter,\" for example, uses the symbol of the letter \"A\" to explore the themes of identity, shame, and the complexity of human emotion and societal judgment.</p>\n\n<p><strong>\"Young Goodman Brown\" and Other Short Stories</strong><br>\nHawthorne's short stories, like \"Young Goodman Brown,\" often focus on the inherent evil and sinfulness of humanity. They typically feature a Young England setting and characters grappling with their consciences.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Brook Farm Community</strong><br>\nIn 1841, Hawthorne joined the Brook Farm community, a utopian experiment in communal living. Although he later left the community, his experience there influenced his views on human nature and society, which he explored in his writing.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
