            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>\"The House of the Seven Gables\"</strong><br>\nAnother of Hawthorne's famous works is \"The House of the Seven Gables,\" published in 1851. The novel uses an ancestral home as a metaphor for the decay of the family line and the impact of the past on the present.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Hawthorne's Family and Personal Life</strong><br>\nHawthorne was married to Sophia Peabody, and they had three children. He often struggled with financial instability but continued to write prolifically, supporting his family through his literary efforts.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Hawthorne's Death and Legacy</strong><br>\nNathaniel Hawthorne died in 1864. His works remain a significant part of the American literary canon and continue to be studied for their exploration of the American identity and moral complexity.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
